}

my $inputs_str = join(',', @input_vns);
return "$op:$inputs_str";
```

So for Start nodes:
- op = 'Start'
- inputs = []
- inputs_str = '' (empty string since no inputs)
- value_number = "Start:"

**All Start nodes with the same function name will get the same value number!**

Wait, but I don't see attributes being included in the value number computation for general operations. The GVN only uses `$op:$inputs_str`, not attributes.

So all 15 Start nodes will have:
- value_number = "Start:"
- They'll all get merged into one!

Similarly, all Return nodes with the same value input will get merged.


I'll investigate the Return nodes carefully. Their value numbers depend on two key inputs: the control input and the value to return. Different control inputs (pointing to distinct intermediate Start nodes) will create unique value numbers. This prevents inappropriate merging of Returns from different contexts.

Constants play a crucial role too. Their value numbers are computed based on their specific value and type, ensuring unique identification. This means Constants with different values won't be merged, maintaining the integrity of the optimization process.

When GVN redirects Start nodes to a canonical Start, something interesting happens. All Return nodes will now share the same canonical start ID and constant ID. This alignment allows them to merge, simplifying the graph structure and enabling more effective optimization.

The process reveals a nuanced interaction between Value Numbering and graph transformation, demonstrating how careful tracking of node inputs can lead to significant code simplification.

